<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Evaluation</h2>

<figure id="S5.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05497v1/0exp_throughput.svg" id="S5.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:457/102;" width="457" height="102"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9. </span>Throughput of MoE layers (scaled to baseline).</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05497v1/0exp_hop.svg" id="S5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/111;" width="467" height="111"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10. </span>Hop number reduction ratio (scaled to baseline).</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experiment Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Methodology: </span>
We developed a custom multi-chiplet GPU simulator in Python due to the lack of suitable existing tools. While cycle-accurate simulators like Gem5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Binkert et al., 2011</a>)</cite>, gpgpusm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bakhoda et al., 2009</a>)</cite>, and mgpusim <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2019</a>)</cite> accurately model single GPUs, they are too slow for large-scale systems with over 20 GPUs serving batch sizes exceeding 15,000. ASTRI-sim <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Won et al., 2023</a>)</cite> supports multi-GPU systems but relies on real system traces and targets traditional LLM training, lacking MoE serving support.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our trace-based simulator models key components including LLC, HBM, Compute Units, and D2D links across all dies while considering hardware resource contention.
The simulator supports configurable die numbers, placement, and connectivity.
To balance accuracy and efficiency, we simulate MoE models at expert slice granularity, with each expert comprising two slices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric: </span> We measure pure MoE layer performance during the decode stage as modern LLM serving systems show a trend toward fine-granularity disaggregation. Traditional LLMs benefit from separating prefill and decode stages across different machines, as demonstrated by DistServe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and subsequent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For MoE models, this disaggregation extends further. MegaScale-Infer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> separates attention and MoE operations onto different machines for optimal batch sizes. Following this trend, we focus on optimizing MoE operations during the decode stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware Configuration: </span>
We evaluate on two multi-chiplet topologies: Tesla Dojo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Talpes et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Talpes et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and TSMC SoW roadmap <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">TSMC, 2025</a>)</cite>. As shown in  <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.4.5. Algorithm Design ‣ 4.4. Architecture Design ‣ 4. Case Study: Future GPUs Architecture Design for MoE Models ‣ Orders in Chaos: Enhancing Large-Scale MoE LLM Serving with Data Movement Forecasting" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 2</span></a>, Tesla Dojo features a 5×5 2D mesh while TSMC SoW employs an 8×3 2D mesh. We selected these configurations as they represent real-world deployment (Tesla Dojo) and near-future industry support (TSMC SoW).
Each chiplet has 1,000 TFLOPS FP8 computing power, 512 GB HBM, 2 TB/s local HBM bandwidth, and 1.5 TB/s interconnection bandwidth between adjacent dies. We reserve 10% of DRAM for hardware management.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Task Allocation Algorithm</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.6.1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
<span id="algorithm1.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span><math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="expert\_reqs\_dict" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">expert\_reqs\_dict</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="expert\_die\_map" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">expert\_die\_map</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"><span id="algorithm1.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> </span><math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="allo\_plan" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">allo\_plan</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">1</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.6.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Initialize</span><span id="algorithm1.6.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> the workload of each die: </span><math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="load\_per\_die" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">load\_per\_die</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm1.6.3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">;</span>
</div>
